        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_1",
          "order": "2",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.486,
            "y": 0.054,
            "width": 0.036,
            "height": 0.012
          },
          "block_content": "KIM",
          "label": "Text"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_2",
          "order": "3",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.078,
            "y": 0.082,
            "width": 0.415,
            "height": 0.048
          },
          "block_content": "\\(B(\\nu)\\) and \\(A(\\nu)\\) are summarized over the science band and science core using medians, percentiles, or simple channel-wise sums, depending on the diagnostic being performed.",
          "label": "Text"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_3",
          "order": "4",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.202,
            "y": 0.141,
            "width": 0.169,
            "height": 0.015
          },
          "block_content": "3.3. Auxiliary Metrics",
          "label": "Text"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_4",
          "order": "5",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.078,
            "y": 0.162,
            "width": 0.415,
            "height": 0.046
          },
          "block_content": "To connect Monte Carlo summaries to scientific impact, we use two additional aggregate metrics. First, we define the integrated absolute error over the science core as",
          "label": "Text"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_5",
          "order": "6",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.17399999999999974,
            "y": 0.2159999999999987,
            "width": 0.22080777537796997,
            "height": 0.031999999999999966
          },
          "block_content": "\\mathrm{IAE}_{\\mathrm{core}} =\n\\sum_{\\nu \\in \\mathrm{core}}\n\\bigl|\\hat{S}(\\nu) - S(\\nu)\\bigr|,\n\\label{eq:iae-core}",
          "label": "Formula"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_6",
          "order": "7",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.078,
            "y": 0.258,
            "width": 0.414,
            "height": 0.046
          },
          "block_content": "which serves as a channel-summed proxy (i.e., a discrete sum over channels, with no explicit  \\( \\Delta\\nu \\)  factor) within the protected region.",
          "label": "Text"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_7",
          "order": "8",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.093,
            "y": 0.305,
            "width": 0.336,
            "height": 0.015
          },
          "block_content": "Second, we define the detection failure rate as",
          "label": "Text"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_8",
          "order": "9",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.2340000000000002,
            "y": 0.32899999999999946,
            "width": 0.10581209503239723,
            "height": 0.03099999999999995
          },
          "block_content": "\\[\np _ {\\text { fail }} = \\frac {N _ {\\text { fail }}}{N _ {\\text { trial }}}, \\tag {8}\n\\]",
          "label": "Formula"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_9",
          "order": "10",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.078,
            "y": 0.369,
            "width": 0.414,
            "height": 0.111
          },
          "block_content": "where  \\( N_{trial} \\)  is the total number of injection–recovery trials and  \\( N_{fail} \\)  is the number of trials in which a Gaussian line fit to the recovered spectrum within the science core either fails to converge or yields a fitted amplitude below the adopted detection threshold. This metric quantifies missed recovery of the injected science feature rather than generic spectral-shape errors.",
          "label": "Text"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_10",
          "order": "11",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.23,
            "y": 0.49,
            "width": 0.112,
            "height": 0.014
          },
          "block_content": "4. METHODS",
          "label": "Text"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_11",
          "order": "12",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.078,
            "y": 0.511,
            "width": 0.414,
            "height": 0.047
          },
          "block_content": "We now define the low-rank cleaning methods considered for a single-epoch matrix  \\( D \\in R^{T \\times F} \\)  and the comparison references.",
          "label": "Text"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_12",
          "order": "13",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.137,
            "y": 0.569,
            "width": 0.297,
            "height": 0.017
          },
          "block_content": "4.1. Frequency-weighted SVD (FWSVD)",
          "label": "Text"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_13",
          "order": "14",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.078,
            "y": 0.59,
            "width": 0.414,
            "height": 0.048
          },
          "block_content": "To suppress variance explanation in the protected channels, we introduce a diagonal weight matrix  \\( W \\in R^{F \\times F} \\)  along the frequency axis. Channel weights are",
          "label": "Text"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_14",
          "order": "15",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.134,
            "y": 0.648,
            "width": 0.357,
            "height": 0.065
          },
          "block_content": "    w_{\\mathrm{core}}, & \\nu \\in \\mathrm{science~core}, \\\\\n    w_{\\mathrm{prot}}, & \\nu \\in \\mathrm{science~band}\\setminus\\mathrm{core}, \\\\\n    1,                 & \\text{otherwise},\n",
          "label": "Formula"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_15",
          "order": "16",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.078,
            "y": 0.724,
            "width": 0.414,
            "height": 0.032
          },
          "block_content": "with  \\( 0 < w_{core} \\leq w_{prot} \\leq 1 \\) , and  \\( W = \\text{diag}(w(\\nu)) \\) . We define the frequency-weighted snapshot as",
          "label": "Text"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_16",
          "order": "17",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.235,
            "y": 0.765,
            "width": 0.255,
            "height": 0.018
          },
          "block_content": "D^{(W)} \\equiv D\\,W,",
          "label": "Formula"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_17",
          "order": "18",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.079,
            "y": 0.794,
            "width": 0.166,
            "height": 0.016
          },
          "block_content": "and compute its SVD,",
          "label": "Text"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_18",
          "order": "19",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.23,
            "y": 0.818,
            "width": 0.26,
            "height": 0.02
          },
          "block_content": "D^{(W)} \\approx \\tilde{U} \\tilde{\\Sigma} \\tilde{V}^\\top .",
          "label": "Formula"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_19",
          "order": "20",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.078,
            "y": 0.848,
            "width": 0.414,
            "height": 0.033
          },
          "block_content": "For rank k, the rank-k approximation in the weighted space is  \\( \\tilde{U}_{1:k}\\tilde{\\Sigma}_{1:k}\\tilde{V}_{1:k}^{\\top} \\) , and we subtract",
          "label": "Text"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_20",
          "order": "21",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.144,
            "y": 0.889,
            "width": 0.346,
            "height": 0.022
          },
          "block_content": "D_{\\mathrm{clean}}^{\\mathrm{(FWSVD)}}(k)\n    = D - \\tilde{U}_{1:k} \\ilde{\\Sigma}_{1:k} \\tilde{V}_{1:k}^\\top W^{-1}.",
          "label": "Formula"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_21",
          "order": "22",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.551,
            "y": 0.083,
            "width": 0.325,
            "height": 0.016
          },
          "block_content": "4.2. Temporally smoothed SVD (TempSVD)",
          "label": "Text"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_22",
          "order": "23",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.504,
            "y": 0.103,
            "width": 0.417,
            "height": 0.108
          },
          "block_content": "To benchmark the effect of imposing temporal regularity on the fitted low-rank component, we include a simple temporally smoothed SVD reference method. Let  \\( K \\in R^{T \\times T} \\)  be a linear smoothing operator acting along time (we use a symmetric boxcar moving-average window of width m samples, normalized to unit row sum). We form the smoothed matrix",
          "label": "Text"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_23",
          "order": "24",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.663,
            "y": 0.224,
            "width": 0.256,
            "height": 0.019
          },
          "block_content": "D^{(\\mathrm{sm})} = K D,",
          "label": "Formula"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_24",
          "order": "25",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.505,
            "y": 0.256,
            "width": 0.415,
            "height": 0.047
          },
          "block_content": "compute its rank-k truncated-SVD approximation  \\( (D^{(\\mathrm{sm})})_{k} \\) , and subtract this fit from the original (unsmoothed) snapshot,",
          "label": "Text"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_25",
          "order": "26",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.597,
            "y": 0.315,
            "width": 0.322,
            "height": 0.022
          },
          "block_content": "D_{\\mathrm{clean}}^{(\\mathrm{TempSVD})}(k) = D - (D^{(\\mathrm{sm})})_k .",
          "label": "Formula"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_26",
          "order": "27",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.504,
            "y": 0.348,
            "width": 0.416,
            "height": 0.078
          },
          "block_content": "The window width m controls the strength of temporal averaging: larger m suppresses fast time variability but can introduce horizontal (time-smeared) residual structure. We treat TempSVD as an illustrative regularized reference method, not as a proposed default.",
          "label": "Text"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_27",
          "order": "28",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.504,
            "y": 0.427,
            "width": 0.416,
            "height": 0.061
          },
          "block_content": "In practice, for TempSVD we use an alternating least-squares solver for the rank-1 case for numerical stability and computational efficiency, while retaining the same conceptual structure.",
          "label": "Text"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_28",
          "order": "29",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.504,
            "y": 0.489,
            "width": 0.416,
            "height": 0.078
          },
          "block_content": "To summarize the trade-off between science distortion and residual RFI contamination, we define two scalar Pareto proxies over the science band. For clarity, ‘residual RFI contamination’ denotes signal remaining after cleaning, not instrumental spectral leakage or ADC artifacts.",
          "label": "Text"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_29",
          "order": "30",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.521,
            "y": 0.568,
            "width": 0.211,
            "height": 0.015
          },
          "block_content": "The science-distortion proxy,",
          "label": "Text"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_30",
          "order": "31",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.611,
            "y": 0.5950000000000001,
            "width": 0.20540820734341247,
            "height": 0.03899999999999998
          },
          "block_content": "\\[\n\\text { Bias } ^ {-} = 1 - \\frac {\\sum_ {\\nu \\in \\text { band }} \\hat {S} (\\nu)}{\\sum_ {\\nu \\in \\text { band }} S (\\nu)}, \\tag {15}\n\\]",
          "label": "Formula"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_31",
          "order": "32",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.504,
            "y": 0.643,
            "width": 0.416,
            "height": 0.093
          },
          "block_content": "measures the signed fractional change of the injected science component after cleaning (Bias \\( ^{-} \\) = 0 for perfect preservation). Negative values correspond to net amplification rather than loss. In Pareto plots we therefore use |Bias \\( ^{-} \\) | as a distortion magnitude, while retaining the sign in tabulated summaries.",
          "label": "Text"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_32",
          "order": "33",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.521,
            "y": 0.738,
            "width": 0.25,
            "height": 0.015
          },
          "block_content": "The residual-contamination proxy,",
          "label": "Text"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_33",
          "order": "34",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.6260000000000002,
            "y": 0.7650000000000006,
            "width": 0.17528941684665209,
            "height": 0.03800000000000004
          },
          "block_content": "\\[\n\\mathrm{Bias} ^ {+} = \\frac {\\sum_ {\\nu \\in \\text { band }} \\hat {I} (\\nu)}{\\sum_ {\\nu \\in \\text { band }} I (\\nu)}, \\tag {16}\n\\]",
          "label": "Formula"
        },
        {
          "id": "pred-11-4-4_34",
          "order": "35",
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.505,
            "y": 0.813,
            "width": 0.417,
            "height": 0.098
          },
          "block_content": "is evaluated using dedicated RFI-only trials in which the science component is set to S = 0, so that  \\( D = I + N \\) . In these trials, the time-averaged residual  \\( \\hat{I}(\\nu) = \\left\\langle D_{\\mathrm{clean}}^{(k)}(t, \\nu) \\right\\rangle_{t} \\)  directly estimates the residual RFI contamination that survives rank-k subtraction. The denominator  \\( \\sum_{\\nu \\in band} I(\\nu) \\)  is the corresponding time-average of the",
          "label": "Text"
        },
        {
          "id": "JZQQTNrezj",
          "order": null,
          "bounding_box": {
            "x": 0.46868250539956796,
            "y": 0.2196830704969799,
            "width": 0.023758099352051833,
            "height": 0.015846538782318592
          },
          "block_content": "(7)",
          "label": "Text"
        },
        {
